/// Chapter 8 of the guide, written from md-docs/08-state-and-reactivity.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page StateGuide(path: "/docs/guide/state", title: "State and reactivity", description: "state, derived, effect, actions and let, persist, timers, clean-up, element handles and the browser's values.", layout: DocsShell("State and reactivity", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "8" }), blurb: "Reactivity here is fine-grained: a state is a signal, and only the thing that read it updates.", source: "md-docs/08-state-and-reactivity.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "state", label: "state"), Anchor(id: "derived", label: "derived"), Anchor(id: "effect", label: "effect"), Anchor(id: "actions-and-locals", label: "Actions and locals"), Anchor(id: "persist", label: "persist"), Anchor(id: "timers", label: "Timers"), Anchor(id: "automatic-clean-up", label: "Automatic clean-up"), Anchor(id: "element-handles", label: "Element handles"), Anchor(id: "the-browser-as-values", label: "The browser as values"), Anchor(id: "common-mistakes", label: "Common mistakes"), Anchor(id: "where-values-come-from-in-order", label: "Where values come from, in order")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/elements", prevLabel: "7. {t("ch.07")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/events", nextLabel: "9. {t("ch.09")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("WebFluent's reactivity is fine-grained: a `state` is a signal, and anything that reads it — a text, an attribute, a `derived` value, an `effect`, a style splice — follows it. There is no re-render of a tree; the thing that read the value is what updates.")

        Heading("state", id: "state").h2

        CodeBlock(#"page Counter(path: "/") {
    state count = 0                  // inferred: Number
    state name: String = ""          // declared
    state items: [String] = []       // a list of strings
    state selected: String? = null   // may be null
    state user = { name: "Sam", tags: ["a"] }   // a map literal: its fields are its type

    Text("{name} has clicked {count} times")
    Button("+") { on click { count = count + 1 } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A `state` is declared at the top of a page, component or store body, with an initial value. Its type is read off the value, or written after a colon; [chapter 13](/docs/guide/types) has the type language. Assigning a value of another type is an error (`T01`).\n\nState is written only in an imperative block — a handler, an action, an effect: `count = count + 1`, `user.name = \"Ann\"`, `items[0] = \"x\"`, or through a method that changes it in place (`items.push(\"x\")`). The static paint seeds every state with its initial value, so a pre-rendered page shows what the first paint would.")

        Heading("derived", id: "derived").h2

        Markdown("A derived value is computed from state and recomputed when any state it read changes:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Cart(path: "/") {
    state items = [{ name: "Pen", price: 2.5, qty: 4 }, { name: "Pad", price: 6, qty: 1 }]
    state taxRate = 0.2
    derived subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0)
    derived total = subtotal * (1 + taxRate)
    derived empty = items.length == 0

    Text("Subtotal: {format(subtotal, .currency)}")
    Text("Total: {format(total, .currency)}")
    if empty { Text("Your cart is empty.").muted }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A derived value may read other derived values and call actions. It is never assigned to. It is seeded in the static paint from the initial state.")

        Heading("effect", id: "effect").h2

        Markdown("An effect runs once, and again whenever a state it read changes. Its `cleanup \{ \}` block runs before the next run and when the page (or the branch the effect lives in) leaves:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Watch(path: "/") {
    state query = ""
    effect {
        log("query is now {query}")
    }
    effect {
        document.title = "Search: {query}"
        cleanup { document.title = "Search" }
    }
    Input(bind: query, placeholder: "Search")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Use effects for the world outside the page — the document title, a third-party widget, a subscription. Keep computation in `derived`.")

        Heading("Actions and locals", id: "actions-and-locals").h2

        Markdown("An action is a named function of the page, component or store. Its body is an imperative block: assignments, `let` locals, calls, `if`, `for`, `try`, `return`, `await`.")

        CodeBlock(#"page Todos(path: "/") {
    state items: [String] = []
    state draft = ""

    action add() {
        let text = draft.trim()
        if text == "" { return }
        items.push(text)
        draft = ""
    }

    action remove(index: Number) {
        items = items.filter((_, i) => i != index)
    }

    action load() {
        let r = await fetch("/api/todos")
        let { todos } = r
        items = todos
    }

    Input(bind: draft, placeholder: "What needs doing?") { on keydown(e) { if e.key == "Enter" { add() } } }
    Button("Add").primary { on click { add() } }
    for item, i in items {
        Row { Text(item)  IconButton(icon: "trash", label: "Remove") { on click { remove(i) } } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- `let x = …` declares a local of the action (or handler). `let \{ a, b \} = m` and `let [x, y] = l` destructure.\n- `await` inside an action makes it asynchronous; `name.pending` is `true` while a call of it runs ([chapter 16](/docs/guide/forms#pending-actions)).\n- `for item in list \{ \}`, `for item, i in list \{ \}` and `for n in 1..=10 \{ \}` loop; `try \{ \} catch e \{ \}` guards a call that may fail; `return x` leaves with a value.\n- A page's action is called by name, `add()`; a store's through the store, `Todos.add()`.")

        Heading("persist", id: "persist").h2

        Markdown("`persist` is a `state` kept in the browser's storage across visits. It is read back on the next visit and written on every change:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Prefs(path: "/") {
    persist theme = "light"
    persist perPage = 20
    Select(bind: theme, label: "Theme") {
        Select.Option("Light", value: "light")
        Select.Option("Dark", value: "dark")
    }
    Slider(bind: perPage, min: 10, max: 100, step: 10, label: "Rows per page")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A `persist` may say more about itself — which storage, a version with migrations, whether to follow other tabs — in a block after it; that and the rest of the policy are in [Stores](/docs/guide/stores#keeping-a-value-across-visits). It works in a store too, where every page shares it. The key is the owner's name and the state's (`Prefs.theme`), so a page and a store may each have a `theme`. Storage that is blocked or full falls back to the initial value.")

        Heading("Timers", id: "timers").h2

        Markdown("`every(ms) \{ \}` repeats; `after(ms) \{ \}` fires once. The interval is a number of milliseconds, or a duration: `every(5.seconds)`, `after(2.minutes)`. Both stop when what declared them leaves the page — a branch that closes, a list item that is removed, a route that changes:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Clock(path: "/") {
    state seconds = 0
    state toast: String? = "Welcome"
    every(1000) { seconds = seconds + 1 }
    after(3000) { toast = null }
    Text("{seconds}s on this page")
    if let t = toast { Alert(t).info }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Automatic clean-up", id: "automatic-clean-up").h2

        Markdown("Everything a body creates — effects, timers, listeners, handles — belongs to the scope it was created in. When that scope's nodes leave (an `if` branch turns false, an item drops out of a `for`, a `match` arm changes, a route changes), the runtime disposes of them: intervals are cleared, effects fall silent, cleanups run. You never unsubscribe by hand.")

        Heading("Element handles", id: "element-handles").h2

        Markdown("`ref: name` on an element gives you a handle, read as the element itself:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Search(path: "/") {
    state q = ""
    Input(bind: q, ref: box, placeholder: "Search")
    Button("Focus the box") { on click { box.focus() } }
    Button("Clear") { on click { q = ""  box.focus() } }
    on key("/") { box.focus() }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The name is declared by its use; `box.value`, `box.scrollIntoView()`, anything the element has.")

        Heading("The browser as values", id: "the-browser-as-values").h2

        Markdown("Some names are always in scope, kept current by the runtime, so a page reads the browser the way it reads its own state:")

        Table(caption: "The browser as values") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Name").header
                    Table.Cell("Is").header
                    Table.Cell("Updates when").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`viewport`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.width`, `.height`, and `.sm` `.md` `.lg` `.xl` — whether the window is at least that breakpoint") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a breakpoint is crossed (through `matchMedia`, not on every pixel)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`query`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the URL's query string as a map: `query.tab` for `?tab=x`") }
                    Table.Cell("the URL changes")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`hash`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the URL's `#fragment`, without the `#`") }
                    Table.Cell("the URL changes")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`theme`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"light\"`, `\"dark\"` or `\"system\"` — the reader's choice ([Styling](/docs/guide/styling#dark-mode))") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`setTheme(…)` is called") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`now`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a `DateTime`, every minute; `now(every: 1.seconds)` for a finer clock ([Types](/docs/guide/types#now))") }
                    Table.Cell("the clock ticks")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`network`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.online`, `.effectiveType`, `.saveData`, `.downlink`, `.queued` ([Real-time](/docs/guide/realtime#the-network-as-a-value))") }
                    Table.Cell("the connection changes")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`update`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.available` and `.apply()` — a new version of an offline site ([Offline](/docs/guide/offline))") }
                    Table.Cell("a new build is installed")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`locale`, `dir`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the current locale and `\"ltr\"`/`\"rtl\"`, with i18n configured ([i18n](/docs/guide/i18n))") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`setLocale(…)` is called") }
                }
            }
        }

        CodeBlock(#"page Where(path: "/", title: "Where", description: "The browser, read as values.") {
    Heading("Where am I?").h1
    Text(if viewport.md { "A wide window" } else { "A narrow window" })
    derived tab = query.tab ?? "all"
    Text("Tab: {tab}, section: {hash}")
    Text("Theme: {theme}")
    if !network.online { Alert("You are offline.").warning }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Breakpoints are the `screen-sm` … `screen-xl` design tokens — 640, 768, 1024 and 1280px unless a theme moves them. A `state` you declare with one of these names shadows it. In the static paint they are unknown, so what depends on them is drawn once the page is live — for layout, prefer [responsive values](/docs/guide/styling#responsive-values), which are right in the first paint.")

        Heading("Common mistakes", id: "common-mistakes").h2

        Markdown("- **Writing state in a `derived`.** A derived value computes; it never assigns. Put the assignment in an action or an `effect`.\n- **An effect that writes what it reads.** `effect \{ n = n + 1 \}` reads `n`, so it runs again, forever. Compute it with `derived` instead.\n- **Mutating a list and expecting a copy.** `items.push(x)` changes `items` in place and notifies its readers; `let copy = items` is the same list. Use `items.slice()` or `[...items]` for a copy.\n- **Reading an element handle during set-up.** A `ref:` is filled once the element exists; read it in a handler, an `effect` or `after(0) \{ \}`.\n- **Expecting a component to re-run.** A component's body runs once. A value that should follow a prop or a state is a `derived`, or an expression written where it is shown.")

        Heading("Where values come from, in order", id: "where-values-come-from-in-order").h2

        Markdown("1. Route parameters and props (`slug`, `_ label`).\n2. `state`, `persist`, `derived`, `resource`, actions of the body.\n3. A `use`d store's members, through the store's name (`Cart.total`).\n4. Loop variables and arm bindings inside their block.\n5. Constants, `data` and `image` declarations, `env.X`, and the browser's values above.\n6. Browser globals: `window`, `document`, `localStorage`, `JSON`, `Math`, `Date`, `fetch`, `setTimeout`, `console`, `navigator`, `location`.")
    }
}
